264
00:19:57,230 --> 00:19:57,755
Speaker 0: Well, weird nerds.

265
00:20:01,812 --> 00:20:08,248
Speaker 0: In other minor news, right, Polygon has an article about this study, right?

266
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:13,622
Speaker 0: I can't actually find the data from the study that they're talking about.

267
00:20:13,682 --> 00:20:15,950
Speaker 0: It might have just been like a study they did themselves.

268
00:20:16,430 --> 00:20:21,430
Speaker 0: Polygon surveyed more than 4,000 Americans over the age of 18 about their anime consumption habits.

269
00:20:21,570 --> 00:20:29,289
Speaker 0: So basically this is the study and they haven't published their raw data that I can find, but they did the study themselves.

270
00:20:29,369 --> 00:20:29,610
Speaker 0: Okay.

271
00:20:30,031 --> 00:20:31,749
Speaker 0: And this is just their report on it.

272
00:20:32,511 --> 00:20:40,410
Speaker 0: And basically their study is that, hey, yes indeed what you thought anime is way more popular than it appears to be.

273
00:20:40,510 --> 00:20:47,950
Speaker 0: And you can read all these numbers yourself because obviously we're going to put the link here on our website and in our notes and everything like that.

274
00:20:49,231 --> 00:20:58,490
Speaker 1: You always like to say where on one hand, duh, obviously I knew this, but at the other hand, there's a difference between feeling like you know something and having data to show it.

275
00:20:59,450 --> 00:21:23,510
Speaker 0: Well, I think the thing is, I've been saying this for a while and I'm more sure of it every day, is that there is a huge, I guess, I had the right word for it and I forgot the right word, but there is a huge separation between how popular we think a thing is or how popular a thing is perceived to be and how popular a thing actually is.

276
00:21:23,870 --> 00:21:33,070
Speaker 1: Well, case in point, people who are fans of, I don't know, random generic TV sitcom, it's not like, there might be hundreds of millions of people watching that sitcom.

277
00:21:33,810 --> 00:21:40,990
Speaker 1: How many of them are going to go to a convention about that sitcom or even about sitcoms or even think to mention it to people in most conversations?

278
00:21:41,232 --> 00:21:42,036
Speaker 1: Like it doesn't come up.

279
00:21:42,399 --> 00:21:54,870
Speaker 0: So the image of an anime fan is- There are things which are popular but do not appear to be popular because there is no talk about them either amongst the people or amongst the media, right?

280
00:21:55,070 --> 00:22:06,190
Speaker 0: There are things that are unpopular that appear to be big and popular because like they're in the movie theater and someone's doing a promo circuit and a lot of money is being pumped into it.

281
00:22:06,272 --> 00:22:06,707
Speaker 1: Marvel comic books.

282
00:22:06,810 --> 00:22:12,539
Speaker 0: There are ads and there are, you know, astroturfs and it seems like it's a big deal, right?

283
00:22:12,599 --> 00:22:20,170
Speaker 0: It's like, you know, you could have something where like one person tweets about a thing and then maybe there's five people who are in agreement.

284
00:22:20,771 --> 00:22:30,329
Speaker 0: And then someone does a news story on it and then every news outlet copies that news story and now what was just five people seems like a fad and a trend and then people get in on it.

285
00:22:30,911 --> 00:22:33,468
Speaker 0: But how many people were ever actually in on it, right?

286
00:22:33,852 --> 00:22:35,190
Speaker 0: That kind of thing can happen.

287
00:22:35,350 --> 00:22:36,092
Speaker 1: That whole Fortnite thing.

288
00:22:36,132 --> 00:22:42,850
Speaker 1: Fortnite made a change and if you looked at the internet, you would believe that everyone who plays Fortnite is up in arms and they're going to quit the game.

289
00:22:43,270 --> 00:22:47,690
Speaker 1: And then when all the people who said they quit were quit, Fortnite was like, yeah, that was basically zero people.

290
00:22:48,810 --> 00:22:48,930
Speaker 0: Right.

291
00:22:49,312 --> 00:22:52,442
Speaker 0: So this is- and sometimes it's actually true.

292
00:22:52,543 --> 00:22:54,710
Speaker 0: Think that seems unpopular is unpopular.

293
00:22:54,992 --> 00:22:55,081
Speaker 0: Yeah.

294
00:22:55,630 --> 00:22:58,630
Speaker 0: And, you know, and think that seems popular is popular, right?

295
00:22:58,690 --> 00:23:03,356
Speaker 1: But anime has definitely been- It's just mainstream, generically popular.

296
00:23:03,396 --> 00:23:09,990
Speaker 1: There are definitely tons of people who watch a lot of anime and would never think to even mention that fact to other people.

297
00:23:12,890 --> 00:23:24,870
Speaker 0: So I think that's just something we had to be aware of is the perception of popularity and the truth of popularity are strongly disconnected in many ways.

298
00:23:27,390 --> 00:23:35,090
Speaker 1: In one last little bit of news, this has been making the rounds for a while, but I just wanted to highlight it because this is one example that's very specific.

299
00:23:35,811 --> 00:23:37,417
Speaker 1: There's a board game called Kelp.

300
00:23:37,759 --> 00:23:38,542
Speaker 1: Looks like a cute game.

301
00:23:38,662 --> 00:23:39,827
Speaker 1: I don't have any strong opinion on it.

302
00:23:39,867 --> 00:23:40,530
Speaker 1: I haven't read the rules.

303
00:23:40,590 --> 00:23:41,169
Speaker 0: It was on Kickstarter.

304
00:23:41,450 --> 00:23:42,030
Speaker 1: Yeah, it was Kickstarter.

305
00:23:42,290 --> 00:23:44,030
Speaker 0: I saw it, but I wasn't interested.

306
00:23:44,531 --> 00:23:58,010
Speaker 1: And because it's a small Kickstarter, it is only available via that Kickstarter or the pledge manager related to the Kickstarting on Kickstarter.

307
00:23:58,210 --> 00:24:06,090
Speaker 1: Like, there's nowhere else to buy it unless you go to the secondary market and buy it from someone who already bought it from that pledge manager from the original Kickstarter.

308
00:24:06,090 --> 00:24:08,946
Speaker 0: But the Kickstarter hasn't printed and shipped yet, so you couldn't buy-.

309
00:24:09,690 --> 00:24:10,631
Speaker 1: Well, yeah, we'll get to that.

310
00:24:10,671 --> 00:24:11,072
Speaker 1: That's true.

311
00:24:11,292 --> 00:24:23,750
Speaker 1: So, despite the game having not actually been delivered to people yet, it appeared on Amazon and eBay and tons of other dodgy websites and got really popular.

312
00:24:24,691 --> 00:24:33,670
Speaker 1: And the people who made it put out a press release saying, and I'm quoting, "At the moment, Kelp can only be purchased on our pledge manager.

313
00:24:34,011 --> 00:24:37,886
Speaker 1: We have discovered counterfeit copies of Kelp on platforms like Amazon, eBay, Frugal,

314
00:24:37,906 --> 00:24:38,468
Speaker 1: worldwide.".

315
00:24:38,950 --> 00:24:43,350
Speaker 1: And it goes on to detail everything they know about this situation.

316
00:24:44,050 --> 00:24:45,775
Speaker 1: And this is not the first.

317
00:24:45,976 --> 00:24:47,139
Speaker 1: This is not going to be the last.

318
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:50,590
Speaker 1: This is going to be a problem that's going to escalate rapidly.

319
00:24:51,470 --> 00:24:57,090
Speaker 1: Because board games like this tend to get printed in a very small number of facilities around the world.

320
00:24:57,951 --> 00:25:10,250
Speaker 1: And it's very easy for anyone involved in that process to just print more or just look at images and recreate a shitty version of the game and then print it at a similar kind of facility.

321
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:11,494
Speaker 1: There's no checks on this stuff.

322
00:25:11,775 --> 00:25:15,910
Speaker 1: It's not like every factory does a full copyright check of every random job that gets sent to them.

323
00:25:17,551 --> 00:25:20,214
Speaker 0: This has been a problem for decades now.

324
00:25:20,575 --> 00:25:21,956
Speaker 0: And this is the reason the U.S.

325
00:25:21,976 --> 00:25:30,928
Speaker 0: has intellectual property law is because they basically created a regime whereby you can have a job where you designed a thing.

326
00:25:33,110 --> 00:25:36,236
Speaker 0: And you thought of the thing, the knowledge work.

327
00:25:36,717 --> 00:25:38,661
Speaker 0: And you wrote a SOG.

328
00:25:38,841 --> 00:25:43,550
Speaker 0: And that knowledge work gets financially rewarded in capitalism.

329
00:25:44,672 --> 00:25:47,360
Speaker 0: And so when you buy an Apple product, what does it always say?

330
00:25:47,561 --> 00:25:50,570
Speaker 0: Designed in California, manufactured in other country.

331
00:25:52,251 --> 00:25:56,220
Speaker 0: Every single Apple product has a little notice in there that says exactly those words.

332
00:25:56,621 --> 00:26:00,370
Speaker 0: And Apple is reaping most of the profits because they designed it.

333
00:26:00,891 --> 00:26:03,937
Speaker 0: But in the real world, there is no.

334
00:26:03,957 --> 00:26:10,250
Speaker 0: if there were no governments, you know, make enforcing laws that made those things happen on purpose.

335
00:26:10,831 --> 00:26:15,559
Speaker 0: People who design things would see no return on their work.

336
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:21,950
Speaker 0: Only people who manufacture things are able to see a return because they're the ones who produce the goods.

337
00:26:22,611 --> 00:26:26,296
Speaker 1: The whoever controls the thing.

338
00:26:26,376 --> 00:26:36,110
Speaker 1: that is scarce about something is the only person who can make money without laws, regulations and society backing up the rest of that process.

339
00:26:37,131 --> 00:26:44,530
Speaker 0: Your only hope as a person who designs a thing, a knowledge worker would be to keep your knowledge hidden until the very last minute.

340
00:26:44,630 --> 00:26:48,164
Speaker 0: And you would go to a factory and say, hey, factory, I have a board game.

341
00:26:48,205 --> 00:26:49,550
Speaker 0: It's guaranteed to be a big seller.

342
00:26:49,590 --> 00:26:50,575
Speaker 0: You've seen my other games.

343
00:26:50,595 --> 00:26:51,399
Speaker 0: They were big sellers.

344
00:26:51,780 --> 00:26:53,870
Speaker 0: I will give you the files for this board game.

345
00:26:54,050 --> 00:27:03,090
Speaker 0: They have never been exposed to the outside world except on my secure enclave in exchange for this pile of money, one shot pile of money.

346
00:27:03,170 --> 00:27:10,110
Speaker 0: And then once those files are out, the whole world is now able to produce your game infinitely and you will not be able to see any monies for it.

347
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:19,010
Speaker 0: That is what it would be like if for people who design things, if there were no governments enforcing any kind of law and capitalism still exists.

348
00:27:19,010 --> 00:27:25,210
Speaker 1: What's specifically happening here is that we've hit a point where this would happen off and on because there's various ways to do it.

349
00:27:25,250 --> 00:27:27,698
Speaker 1: But nowadays, partly because there's print and plays.

350
00:27:28,039 --> 00:27:31,550
Speaker 1: People will post screenshots and videos of a board game before it comes out.

351
00:27:32,630 --> 00:27:36,278
Speaker 1: People can get images or guess at the parts they can't see.

352
00:27:36,799 --> 00:27:41,610
Speaker 1: These aren't even just like the factory, like someone took the factory files and printed more.

353
00:27:42,671 --> 00:27:45,796
Speaker 1: Some of these counterfeits are like badly put together.

354
00:27:45,816 --> 00:27:46,797
Speaker 1: They have errors.

355
00:27:46,837 --> 00:27:55,050
Speaker 1: They were clearly made by hand by people who are dedicated to finding board games they can make black market copies of and just making them.

356
00:27:55,190 --> 00:28:03,010
Speaker 1: Like there's all these screenshots of the absolute garbage that has showed up in the mail of people who bought the game who didn't even think they were pirating.

357
00:28:03,230 --> 00:28:04,881
Speaker 1: They thought they were buying the game legitimately.

358
00:28:04,902 --> 00:28:06,110
Speaker 1: They weren't even trying to pirate.

359
00:28:08,431 --> 00:28:16,190
Speaker 0: We've had lots of counterfeit board games for years now, but this is a new one in that the game isn't even out yet.

360
00:28:16,690 --> 00:28:18,773
Speaker 0: That's what makes this one a different situation.

361
00:28:18,853 --> 00:28:29,090
Speaker 0: Somehow the files were recreated or stolen or hoisted in some fashion, leaked at a factory or stolen from the Kickstarter or somehow.

362
00:28:30,812 --> 00:28:37,490
Speaker 0: Who knows if the counterfeit game is even playable, but they're printing it and selling it regardless.

363
00:28:39,031 --> 00:28:40,153
Speaker 1: But I guess be ready.

364
00:28:40,233 --> 00:28:49,950
Speaker 1: This is the tiniest tip of an iceberg that's about to crash across the entire tabletop world except for those games that have minis for now.

365
00:28:51,113 --> 00:28:57,950
Speaker 0: Well, I think that this is going to scare people who make tabletop games into doing things a bit differently.

366
00:28:58,611 --> 00:28:59,694
Speaker 1: Can't do print and plays.

367
00:28:59,995 --> 00:29:04,910
Speaker 1: You definitely can't let anyone have enough assets to make your game in tabletop simulator.

368
00:29:06,130 --> 00:29:18,450
Speaker 0: Well, that's one person suggested that maybe what happened is since they had like a tabletop simulator demo that someone might have gone and taken the assets from the tabletop simulator and use that to create the counterfeit.

369
00:29:20,252 --> 00:29:34,650
Speaker 1: If a game exists in tabletop simulator, which itself is a dubious application in terms of legality and ethical use of other people's intellectual property, we sidestep all that even if somehow tabletop simulator was super secure.

370
00:29:35,410 --> 00:29:39,070
Speaker 1: Analog whole, someone could play the game, take screenshots and print their shitty counterfeit.

371
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:46,343
Speaker 1: But anyway, things of the day.

