Speaker 1: And that's how you control your twit.

574
00:42:26,490 --> 00:42:29,336
Speaker 0: Well, two people wear the hat simultaneously, right?

575
00:42:29,416 --> 00:42:35,690
Speaker 0: So it's two humans with a giant hat that is suspended from like a trestle of some kind.

576
00:42:35,850 --> 00:42:36,091
Speaker 0: Right.

577
00:42:36,532 --> 00:42:42,550
Speaker 0: And two humans strap their heads into this hat and they give you a skullcap because otherwise it'd be gross.

578
00:42:43,571 --> 00:42:49,830
Speaker 0: And then you have to cooperatively use this hat to control what is happening on the screen.

579
00:42:50,731 --> 00:42:56,770
Speaker 0: The line to play the game was long, but I don't feel like I needed to play it to understand what was happening when I looked at it.

580
00:42:56,770 --> 00:43:00,063
Speaker 1: I saw 10 seconds of gameplay and yeah, I'm glad that was there.

581
00:43:00,164 --> 00:43:01,489
Speaker 1: That's a cool thing that someone made.

582
00:43:02,931 --> 00:43:05,529
Speaker 0: Now that's not a thing that anyone's going to be like selling, right?

583
00:43:05,670 --> 00:43:12,030
Speaker 1: Yeah, you're not going to buy the big hat game on Steam and then like take up half the room in your house just to put the big hat.

584
00:43:14,052 --> 00:43:16,830
Speaker 0: It's the kind of thing you could put in like a museum as an installation.

585
00:43:17,330 --> 00:43:18,213
Speaker 1: Yeah, it's exactly that.

586
00:43:18,233 --> 00:43:23,670
Speaker 1: That's why we keep saying these are hybrids of games and installation art.

587
00:43:24,171 --> 00:43:25,477
Speaker 1: And there's a movement like this.

588
00:43:25,517 --> 00:43:28,270
Speaker 1: There is a movement here that is centered around Magfest.

589
00:43:29,411 --> 00:43:34,046
Speaker 0: Now there are some games that could be installations, but some that you could just game.

590
00:43:34,487 --> 00:43:35,270
Speaker 0: Some you can even get.

591
00:43:35,570 --> 00:43:38,820
Speaker 1: Like one that I played that I'm pretty into.

592
00:43:38,840 --> 00:43:42,250
Speaker 1: You know there's that weird Zelda 2 remake where someone just like redesigned the game.

593
00:43:42,790 --> 00:43:44,496
Speaker 1: This one is actually going to be for sale.

594
00:43:45,059 --> 00:43:48,110
Speaker 1: It is the Transylvania Adventure of Simon Quest.

595
00:43:49,510 --> 00:43:52,550
Speaker 1: And it's basically the same as that Zelda 2 situation.

596
00:43:52,730 --> 00:44:03,550
Speaker 1: Someone just took the essence of Castlevania 2 Simon's Quest, but re-implemented it better and avoided compromising intellectual properties so they could actually sell it.

597
00:44:04,230 --> 00:44:18,750
Speaker 1: And I wasn't able to play it until about 2.30 in the morning one night when for the first time in the entire con, the church pew that they had set up in front of the coffins, the real coffins that they had in their little booth.

598
00:44:19,231 --> 00:44:21,758
Speaker 1: Props to them for getting a real church pew and coffins.

599
00:44:22,541 --> 00:44:25,910
Speaker 1: Did not have anyone sitting at it and I played it for like an hour and it was great.

600
00:44:26,390 --> 00:44:28,249
Speaker 1: And I'm definitely going to buy it and play it for real.

601
00:44:31,911 --> 00:44:33,073
Speaker 0: There was an NES game.

602
00:44:33,454 --> 00:44:36,399
Speaker 0: It was like a game that someone actually made for the NES.

603
00:44:36,559 --> 00:44:41,828
Speaker 0: There's a whole section actually in there of games that people have made for old consoles.

604
00:44:43,833 --> 00:44:45,518
Speaker 0: That run on those old consoles.

605
00:44:45,538 --> 00:44:49,050
Speaker 0: There's nothing stopping you from making a new game that's an NES game.

606
00:44:50,590 --> 00:44:54,690
Speaker 0: But obviously are new and not from the olden days.

607
00:44:55,270 --> 00:45:03,527
Speaker 0: And it was just this cute little game where you pick a character and your character has some kind of melee weapon, sort of like a knife.

608
00:45:03,607 --> 00:45:04,108
Speaker 0: it looked like.

609
00:45:04,148 --> 00:45:04,850
Speaker 0: I couldn't really tell.

610
00:45:06,170 --> 00:45:09,496
Speaker 0: But at the bottom of the screen is like popcorn.

611
00:45:10,438 --> 00:45:11,420
Speaker 0: Like a popcorn bag.

612
00:45:11,961 --> 00:45:16,370
Speaker 0: And all these popcorn kernel guys come and try to attack you.

613
00:45:17,231 --> 00:45:21,623
Speaker 0: And if you are touching them, then your health bar is decreasing.

614
00:45:21,723 --> 00:45:23,889
Speaker 0: And when you're not touching them, your health bar goes up.

615
00:45:24,750 --> 00:45:27,980
Speaker 0: If you have your weapon out, your health bar goes down.

616
00:45:28,281 --> 00:45:31,210
Speaker 0: And if you keep your weapon sheathed, your health bar goes up.

617
00:45:31,270 --> 00:45:33,919
Speaker 0: So you can't just run around holding your weapon out all the time.

618
00:45:33,959 --> 00:45:36,085
Speaker 0: You have to strategically stab quickly.

619
00:45:36,126 --> 00:45:37,390
Speaker 0: Like stab, stab, stab.

620
00:45:38,171 --> 00:45:41,964
Speaker 0: And when you stab these guys, they pop into popcorn and fall to the bottom.

621
00:45:42,807 --> 00:45:43,810
Speaker 0: Some of them are blinking.

622
00:45:44,451 --> 00:45:53,770
Speaker 0: If you pop a blinking guy, then he'll turn into a popcorn and any bad guy that popcorn touches on its way down will form a chain reaction of popping.

623
00:45:55,072 --> 00:45:56,034
Speaker 0: And you get a bunch of points.

624
00:45:56,835 --> 00:45:57,496
Speaker 0: That's the whole game.

625
00:45:58,879 --> 00:46:05,330
Speaker 0: The game was surprisingly clever and interesting given the simple concept.

626
00:46:06,810 --> 00:46:10,097
Speaker 0: I just kept playing it even though there wasn't more going on.

627
00:46:10,838 --> 00:46:17,130
Speaker 0: I found an easy strategy to rack up a high score going back and forth off the edges of the screen Pac-Man style.

628
00:46:17,650 --> 00:46:19,596
Speaker 0: Because the bad guys just come right at you.

629
00:46:19,777 --> 00:46:21,161
Speaker 0: They don't have any sort of intelligence.

630
00:46:21,663 --> 00:46:23,328
Speaker 0: So I'm on the left side of the screen.

631
00:46:23,348 --> 00:46:24,030
Speaker 0: They all run left.

632
00:46:24,250 --> 00:46:25,613
Speaker 0: I switch to the right side of the screen.

633
00:46:25,633 --> 00:46:28,099
Speaker 0: They all turn around and come the other way.

634
00:46:28,119 --> 00:46:32,890
Speaker 0: And then eventually I pop one guy who makes a chain reaction and sort of clear things out so it's safe.

635
00:46:33,611 --> 00:46:36,096
Speaker 0: And then I just kept doing that for a long time.

636
00:46:37,058 --> 00:46:39,984
Speaker 0: And then eventually the pressure did mount enough.

637
00:46:40,044 --> 00:46:42,930
Speaker 0: I thought it would sort of cap out and I would be able to play forever.

638
00:46:43,552 --> 00:46:49,608
Speaker 0: But eventually I did, I guess through lack of endurance or increasing number of bad guys, I died.

639
00:46:49,888 --> 00:46:50,430
Speaker 0: And that was that.

640
00:46:51,771 --> 00:46:56,321
Speaker 0: The person who made the game was there and we discussed and recognized that fact.

641
00:46:56,782 --> 00:46:57,905
Speaker 0: And I said, "You know what you should do?

642
00:46:58,767 --> 00:46:59,990
Speaker 0: You should make the popcorn go

643
00:47:00,110 --> 00:47:00,170
Speaker 0: up.".

644
00:47:00,791 --> 00:47:07,065
Speaker 0: I thought the popcorn was going to go up and create sort of a Bomberman-like pressure where the arena shrinks.

645
00:47:08,648 --> 00:47:09,550
Speaker 0: But that never happened.

646
00:47:10,071 --> 00:47:12,599
Speaker 0: And I was like, I was expecting this to happen and it didn't happen.

647
00:47:12,619 --> 00:47:13,021
Speaker 0: And they said,

648
00:47:13,041 --> 00:47:14,625
Speaker 0: "Uhhh.".

649
00:47:15,408 --> 00:47:16,010
Speaker 0: And that was that.

650
00:47:16,750 --> 00:47:19,516
Speaker 0: And I'm sad that I can't remember the name of the game.

651
00:47:19,676 --> 00:47:24,246
Speaker 0: I took a photo of my high score and didn't take a photo of the game.

652
00:47:25,388 --> 00:47:26,230
Speaker 0: Of what game it was.

653
00:47:26,510 --> 00:47:29,378
Speaker 1: A game that we played together and I played several times.

654
00:47:29,658 --> 00:47:34,090
Speaker 1: It actually was made by a former coworker of Emily's because they both worked at the same game studio.

655
00:47:34,770 --> 00:47:35,972
Speaker 1: Nothing good can come of this.

656
00:47:35,993 --> 00:47:46,030
Speaker 1: It has some big Nidhogg vibes in that it's one of those extremely tight, like knife fight in a phone booth kind of symmetric direct competition games.

657
00:47:46,250 --> 00:47:48,250
Speaker 0: It's a gun fight in a phone booth to be more precise.

658
00:47:48,290 --> 00:47:48,972
Speaker 1: In this case true.

659
00:47:49,353 --> 00:47:53,483
Speaker 1: So basically you've got two little guys standing in a very small space and there's a hole in the ceiling.

660
00:47:53,824 --> 00:47:55,688
Speaker 1: And you can punch a little bit and you can jump.

661
00:47:55,708 --> 00:47:56,109
Speaker 1: and that's it.

662
00:47:56,591 --> 00:47:59,156
Speaker 1: You punch each other, just knock each other back a little bit and you can jump.

663
00:47:59,877 --> 00:48:02,402
Speaker 1: And after a few seconds, a gun falls.

664
00:48:03,724 --> 00:48:06,530
Speaker 1: And then a few more seconds later, a bullet falls.

665
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:13,510
Speaker 1: And you can only get a point by holding the gun and the bullet at the same time and successfully shooting the other person.

666
00:48:14,490 --> 00:48:16,353
Speaker 0: But you try to shoot the other person.

667
00:48:17,075 --> 00:48:25,430
Speaker 0: If they're too close to you, the gun will actually be behind them and it's like you'll be holding the gun and using their shoulder as an armrest and shoot behind them.

668
00:48:25,811 --> 00:48:30,021
Speaker 0: You have to be far enough away from them that the gun is gonna actually hit them.

669
00:48:30,201 --> 00:48:31,384
Speaker 0: And that is not so easy.

670
00:48:34,170 --> 00:48:35,355
Speaker 1: But if you're holding the gun, you can't punch.

671
00:48:35,375 --> 00:48:38,950
Speaker 1: So if you're holding the gun, the other person's got the bullet, you gotta throw the gun and try to hit them.

672
00:48:39,290 --> 00:48:42,019
Speaker 1: But if you mess that up, they might pick up the gun and they already have the bullet.

673
00:48:43,082 --> 00:48:45,550
Speaker 1: It's a really tight game and it just forces you to win.

674
00:48:46,011 --> 00:48:48,235
Speaker 1: You've got to get over the line.

675
00:48:48,576 --> 00:48:50,459
Speaker 1: It's like a really hard, tight duel.

676
00:48:50,499 --> 00:48:55,930
Speaker 1: It feels a lot like Nidhogg and that is a genre similar to Outlaw that I'm just a fan of in general.

677
00:48:56,290 --> 00:48:57,231
Speaker 1: I love that genre.

678
00:48:57,752 --> 00:49:18,407
Speaker 1: But the cool thing is, they had it set up in an arcade machine and part of what has helped this creative explosion is just in the last few years, there are multiple companies and multiple avenues to very easily build, buy, or otherwise craft full-size arcade machines that have arbitrary stuff inside of them.

679
00:49:18,427 --> 00:49:19,630
Speaker 1: Like it's got the barrier entrance.

680
00:49:19,690 --> 00:49:25,780
Speaker 0: All you really gotta do is get some wood and if you have woodworking, you can make the cabinet.

681
00:49:26,160 --> 00:49:32,030
Speaker 0: And then inside the cabinet, all you need to put is a Raspberry Pi and a screen and a power cable.

682
00:49:32,070 --> 00:49:35,820
Speaker 0: And then controls, you can just buy joysticks and buttons anywhere.

683
00:49:35,981 --> 00:49:39,250
Speaker 0: And there you go, you got yourself an arcade cabinet decorated how you would.

684
00:49:40,312 --> 00:49:43,270
Speaker 1: There was a Black Emperor and I did get to play Black Emperor.

685
00:49:43,610 --> 00:49:44,452
Speaker 0: It was the Black Emperor.

686
00:49:44,472 --> 00:49:51,170
Speaker 0: I gotta say Black Emperor, now having played it, if you don't know, it was the Omega-Thon finale at PAX West.

687
00:49:53,731 --> 00:49:55,934
Speaker 0: The controls on it are extremely tight and good.

688
00:49:56,014 --> 00:49:56,896
Speaker 0: Perfect, basically.

689
00:49:57,877 --> 00:50:02,124
Speaker 0: It is not actually a hard game at all, control-wise.

690
00:50:02,164 --> 00:50:04,007
Speaker 0: You can perfectly control that bicycle.

691
00:50:04,027 --> 00:50:06,110
Speaker 0: I got the hang of it in like two seconds, motorcycle.

692
00:50:07,390 --> 00:50:17,410
Speaker 0: The only challenge in that game, it is the challenge of memorization because the things come at you so fast that no human could react to most of the changes.

693
00:50:17,410 --> 00:50:20,254
Speaker 0: Because when sand comes at you, you have to hit the throttle harder.

694
00:50:20,695 --> 00:50:30,970
Speaker 0: And when the road turns, you have to turn and it comes at you so fast, you can't possibly react fast enough to not die no matter where you position yourself on the screen.

695
00:50:31,571 --> 00:50:37,287
Speaker 0: So you have to just memorize what's coming up next because it is set in stone, at least until it changes.

696
00:50:39,310 --> 00:50:44,330
Speaker 1: It's the same game as Kaboom for the most part, just with a radically different style.

697
00:50:44,430 --> 00:50:45,875
Speaker 0: Effectively speaking, yes.

698
00:50:46,317 --> 00:50:48,444
Speaker 0: It's Kaboom, but there's also a button you have to regulate.

699
00:50:48,464 --> 00:50:50,270
Speaker 0: Kaboom, you only have to regulate the wheel.

700
00:50:51,210 --> 00:50:55,190
Speaker 1: Speaking of memorization, so Emily and I played a game called Bunny Beats by Studio Tapioca.

701
00:50:55,892 --> 00:50:56,509
Speaker 0: Oh, I saw that one.

702
00:50:56,650 --> 00:50:59,129
Speaker 1: It's got really cute art, but... It's a rhythm game.

703
00:50:59,431 --> 00:51:02,160
Speaker 1: It's a rhythm game, but it's a rhythm game that doesn't fuck around.

704
00:51:02,441 --> 00:51:03,385
Speaker 1: We played it on Easy.

705
00:51:03,445 --> 00:51:03,706
Speaker 1: It is hard.

706
00:51:05,610 --> 00:51:21,870
Speaker 1: I appreciate it in that it is a rhythm game that someone who is good at music and understands rhythm and possibly is a percussionist would love, but someone who does not have rhythm and is not good at music could not play it.

707
00:51:22,632 --> 00:51:24,016
Speaker 1: It basically is a bunch of bunnies.

708
00:51:24,417 --> 00:51:27,185
Speaker 1: They're making mochis, so they're hammering the mochi.

709
00:51:27,506 --> 00:51:28,890
Speaker 1: It's really cute and there's a bunch of them.

710
00:51:29,350 --> 00:51:34,130
Speaker 1: And they're all playing basically a different part of what is a percussion song.

711
00:51:35,130 --> 00:51:50,530
Speaker 1: And the way the game works, it'll show you one of the bunnies will be highlighted, and you have to pay attention to just that bunny and see what part the bunny is playing, memorize it and learn it, having heard it only once.

712
00:51:51,232 --> 00:51:57,390
Speaker 1: And then, in the next stanza of the music, play that bunny's part without any notes or any prompts or any help.

713
00:51:58,550 --> 00:52:00,374
Speaker 0: Yep, this is the middle challenge, right?

714
00:52:00,434 --> 00:52:03,000
Speaker 0: Because you think, "Oh, I could be a percussionist.

715
00:52:03,060 --> 00:52:03,701
Speaker 0: It's so easy,

716
00:52:03,762 --> 00:52:03,942
Speaker 0: right?".

717
00:52:05,044 --> 00:52:07,530
Speaker 0: Some people cannot keep a beat or a rhythm whatsoever.

718
00:52:07,952 --> 00:52:11,026
Speaker 0: I, at least, believe that I can keep a beat or a rhythm.

719
00:52:11,047 --> 00:52:12,292
Speaker 1: The problem is... I've seen you play DDR.

720
00:52:12,312 --> 00:52:18,030
Speaker 1: You definitely can keep a beat or a rhythm, and you can definitely follow instructions on a screen of when to step where.

721
00:52:18,210 --> 00:52:21,876
Speaker 0: I can keep exactly one beat, a rhythm, right?

722
00:52:22,578 --> 00:52:23,419
Speaker 0: At a time, right?

723
00:52:23,479 --> 00:52:26,484
Speaker 0: I can be like, you know, one, two, one, two, one, two, right?

724
00:52:26,745 --> 00:52:29,950
Speaker 0: To be an actual drummer, you have to be able to have one hand...

725
00:52:29,950 --> 00:52:32,330
Speaker 1: Polyrhythm, three on one hand, four on the other hand simultaneously.

726
00:52:33,310 --> 00:52:38,790
Speaker 0: Right, you have to have one hand doing one rhythm and the other hand doing a completely different rhythm, right?

727
00:52:40,811 --> 00:52:47,306
Speaker 0: Not even one hand going one, two, one, two, and the other hand going one, two, three, four, where they're sort of sharing, right?

728
00:52:47,527 --> 00:52:48,910
Speaker 0: That's still the same rhythm.

729
00:52:49,571 --> 00:52:51,643
Speaker 0: We're talking about completely different.

730
00:52:51,784 --> 00:52:52,629
Speaker 0: One hand is going like...

731
00:52:57,791 --> 00:53:08,230
Speaker 1: But it's not like most rhythm games that even have memorization components where it shows you the rhythm, or it shows you the thing, and then you do the thing, and it's kind of synthetic.

732
00:53:08,571 --> 00:53:15,866
Speaker 1: This is more like you're going to hear someone play something, and then you've got to play the same thing in context, and it's a skill that musicians have.

733
00:53:16,247 --> 00:53:17,590
Speaker 1: I don't think anyone else has that skill.

734
00:53:18,510 --> 00:53:20,633
Speaker 0: Right, this is in this sort of middle area.

735
00:53:20,713 --> 00:53:34,950
Speaker 0: It's not asking you to do the full challenge of two simultaneous different rhythms, but it is asking you to do a rhythm while other rhythms are in your ears that aren't the one you're going to play, right?

736
00:53:35,671 --> 00:53:41,956
Speaker 0: And the one you are going to play is not being hinted at, right, while you are playing it visually.

737
00:53:42,217 --> 00:53:56,910
Speaker 0: You have to have memorized it from having heard it sort of like Simon, but it's like if you were four Simons, and you had to reproduce just one of the Simons, and the other four were beeping, the other ones were beeping away at you, right?

738
00:53:57,310 --> 00:54:00,144
Speaker 0: At the same time, it's like that's what this game is.

739
00:54:00,868 --> 00:54:01,330
Speaker 0: Pretty clever.

740
00:54:01,550 --> 00:54:13,470
Speaker 1: But the reason why it's not just impossible is that you're playing the rhythm in the context of a legitimate real song, so it'd be like someone who's an artist- Your rhythm does fit with the other ones.

741
00:54:14,091 --> 00:54:21,026
Speaker 1: Yeah, and picking up on how it fits and what it's doing, which, again, I don't think anyone who is not a musician is capable of.

742
00:54:22,088 --> 00:54:23,010
Speaker 1: That's the challenge.

743
00:54:25,591 --> 00:54:34,650
Speaker 0: Another game that I saw and played, which is available on itch.io at blairzang.itch.io/bbb, is called Bamboom Blade.

744
00:54:35,051 --> 00:54:36,550
Speaker 1: Oh, that's real good.

745
00:54:37,510 --> 00:54:38,311
Speaker 0: This is real simple.

746
00:54:38,372 --> 00:54:48,450
Speaker 0: So basically, it's trying to simulate, you know, kung fu movie wuxia sword fight, you know, situations, sort of like Crouching Tiger Hidden Dragon style.

747
00:54:49,091 --> 00:55:01,630
Speaker 0: There are two very tall bamboo trunks, and on each trunk, there is a warrior person with a sword and nothing else, and they're holding on to that bamboo trunk.

748
00:55:02,391 --> 00:55:03,975
Speaker 0: And the controls are very simple.

749
00:55:04,056 --> 00:55:04,838
Speaker 0: It's twin stick.

750
00:55:05,440 --> 00:55:08,450
Speaker 0: The left stick allows you to move up and down the trunk.

751
00:55:09,311 --> 00:55:19,350
Speaker 0: And the second stick- well, that same- the left stick also, if you move left or right, will allow you to bend the bamboo trunk to the left or right, basically shifting your weight.

752
00:55:19,851 --> 00:55:22,760
Speaker 0: So you can whip yourself around on this trunk like,

753
00:55:22,780 --> 00:55:24,245
Speaker 0: "Ooh-wee!".

754
00:55:24,988 --> 00:55:25,670
Speaker 0: It's a 2D game.

755
00:55:26,891 --> 00:55:29,879
Speaker 0: The right stick only controls your sword.

756
00:55:29,959 --> 00:55:32,005
Speaker 0: If it aims up, you'll hold your sword in the sky.

757
00:55:32,025 --> 00:55:34,030
Speaker 0: If you aim it down, you'll put your sword down.

758
00:55:34,090 --> 00:55:42,610
Speaker 0: If you spin the stick around in a circle, you'll spin your stick- sword around like a, you know, whirlwind, like a- like a fan that's gonna chew up something that gets thrown into it, right?

759
00:55:44,311 --> 00:55:51,990
Speaker 0: And so, there is someone else on the other stick with the same controls, and you are trying to stab them to death without being stabbed to death.

760
00:55:53,391 --> 00:56:00,370
Speaker 0: The rules of this game are quite fascinating in that I found it wasn't quite Bushido Blade, where any touch of the sword kills someone.

761
00:56:01,091 --> 00:56:05,338
Speaker 0: It's very smart with locations and physics and speed.

762
00:56:05,759 --> 00:56:12,350
Speaker 0: Like, for example, in one game, I came together with my opponent, my sword went right into their leg.

763
00:56:12,671 --> 00:56:14,675
Speaker 0: Like, right into it, and their blood was coming out.

764
00:56:14,716 --> 00:56:15,758
Speaker 0: Gary- I knew I hit him.

765
00:56:16,299 --> 00:56:19,266
Speaker 0: Their knee hit me in the head, and I died.

766
00:56:19,727 --> 00:56:20,810
Speaker 0: The sword did not touch me.

767
00:56:22,610 --> 00:56:24,333
Speaker 0: So, that's the kind of game it is.

768
00:56:24,353 --> 00:56:42,390
Speaker 0: If you want to have a two-player fighting game that has a really low barrier to entry, but really sort of high skill cap and possibilities, it's obviously not gonna take you as high as an actual fighting game, like a Street Fighter kind of situation, but you can get a lot of play out of this one.

769
00:56:42,410 --> 00:56:45,276
Speaker 0: And I think you can even get more play out of this than, like, a dive kick.

770
00:56:47,120 --> 00:56:48,724
Speaker 0: So, I appreciated it very much.

771
00:56:48,744 --> 00:56:51,390
Speaker 0: I don't even think they're asking any money.

772
00:56:52,330 --> 00:56:56,846
Speaker 0: One that stood out- Yeah, I pushed download, and it didn't even ask me for money.

773
00:56:56,886 --> 00:56:57,990
Speaker 0: It just, like, starts downloading.

774
00:56:58,530 --> 00:56:59,275
Speaker 1: We'll get in on that.

775
00:56:59,315 --> 00:57:03,191
Speaker 1: That had to be in Outlaw 2 for us, Nidhogg 2.

776
00:57:03,191 --> 00:57:07,370
Speaker 0: It's very similar to Outlaw, just a one-on-one, completely symmetric situation.

777
00:57:07,711 --> 00:57:11,602
Speaker 1: So, I forget the exact title of this game, but it's, like, Bad Art Forgeries.

778
00:57:11,622 --> 00:57:13,988
Speaker 1: It was a really cute little thing.

779
00:57:14,008 --> 00:57:14,650
Speaker 1: It was just set up.

780
00:57:14,770 --> 00:57:20,610
Speaker 1: And basically, you get in the middle of the screen, there's a pixel representation of a famous painting.

781
00:57:21,510 --> 00:57:24,054
Speaker 1: And it's a versus art forgery game.

782
00:57:24,074 --> 00:57:36,050
Speaker 1: You have a very limited amount of time to, with a mouse and a very coarse MS paint set of brushes, recreate that painting as fast as possible.

783
00:57:36,431 --> 00:57:41,224
Speaker 1: The winner is whoever gets the most pixels correct by the end of the timer.

784
00:57:41,826 --> 00:57:42,307
Speaker 1: I won.

785
00:57:42,328 --> 00:57:43,210
Speaker 1: I beat Emily.

786
00:57:44,150 --> 00:57:45,915
Speaker 1: Our paintings look very different from each other.

787
00:57:46,757 --> 00:57:48,140
Speaker 1: My percentage was 62.3.

788
00:57:48,140 --> 00:57:49,202
Speaker 1: I got 62.3% of the pixels correct.

789
00:57:52,830 --> 00:57:55,199
Speaker 1: And Emily got 61.92% of the pixels correct.

790
00:57:58,790 --> 00:58:01,923
Speaker 0: It's literally counting how many pixels are the correct color.

791
00:58:02,506 --> 00:58:03,470
Speaker 0: That's all the game is.

792
00:58:03,711 --> 00:58:04,515
Speaker 1: It's great.

793
00:58:04,616 --> 00:58:07,210
Speaker 1: It is real fun, and it's a really clever idea.

794
00:58:08,290 --> 00:58:14,930
Speaker 0: Okay, the absolute best game that I saw that I don't want to miss is a game called QuestFast, a customer copy adventure.

795
00:58:15,070 --> 00:58:18,390
Speaker 1: Oh my god, that was one of the most impressive things in that whole space.

796
00:58:18,450 --> 00:58:19,413
Speaker 0: It is the best one.

797
00:58:19,594 --> 00:58:24,470
Speaker 0: So, this game, game-wise, is just an adventure game, right?

798
00:58:24,871 --> 00:58:30,610
Speaker 0: Like a text adventure, or a Queen's Quest, or a Monkey Island, or a Shadow Gate, right?

799
00:58:30,650 --> 00:58:36,168
Speaker 0: Where it's like, look at the room, go north, use this item on that thing.

800
00:58:36,208 --> 00:58:36,910
Speaker 0: That's the game.

801
00:58:37,430 --> 00:58:39,557
Speaker 0: There's nothing special about it in that regard.

802
00:58:39,978 --> 00:58:43,950
Speaker 0: It's basically a text adventure where you're going to make breakfast, right?

803
00:58:44,030 --> 00:58:50,795
Speaker 0: Get eggs, get milk, crack eggs into bowl, turn on stove, put eggs in pan- You're trying to make breakfast.

804
00:58:51,197 --> 00:58:52,001
Speaker 0: That's the whole game.

805
00:58:52,463 --> 00:58:53,830
Speaker 0: Nothing else to it, right?

806
00:58:54,653 --> 00:58:58,770
Speaker 0: What makes this game special is the interface for the game.

807
00:58:59,590 --> 00:59:14,910
Speaker 0: They created an entire cash register with a 10-key numpad, a little LCD display with two rows of text, two receipt printers, and a barcode scanner, as well as some markers and a little bulletin board you can use.

808
00:59:15,591 --> 00:59:24,270
Speaker 0: And what happens is there's one receipt printer that is the main one, and that one is basically printing this never-ending long receipt.

809
00:59:24,971 --> 00:59:29,390
Speaker 0: And that never-ending long receipt is actually the main output of the game.

810
00:59:29,970 --> 00:59:42,965
Speaker 0: It says, like, if you tell it, "Look at the plate," then it prints, and you'll see the plate, and you'll see a description of the plate, and it confirms, "The action you chose was to look at plate.

811
00:59:43,447 --> 00:59:43,989
Speaker 0: Here's what you

812
00:59:44,049 --> 00:59:44,230
Speaker 0: saw.".

813
00:59:44,732 --> 00:59:47,621
Speaker 0: And that's the output, right?

814
00:59:48,183 --> 00:59:50,390
Speaker 0: There's also a side receipt printer.

815
00:59:50,831 --> 01:00:04,010
Speaker 0: Whenever you acquire an item, it prints out a tiny little receipt and cuts it, and you get a little slip of paper that has that item and also a barcode on it that represents that item.

816
01:00:04,670 --> 01:00:08,229
Speaker 0: There's also, I think, like, seven barcodes that are, like, always just there.

817
01:00:08,870 --> 01:00:13,670
Speaker 0: Like, those are for the main actions, like, you know, look, take, eat, et cetera.

818
01:00:14,091 --> 01:00:23,615
Speaker 0: So you would scan on the look barcode, which is always there, and scan the plate, the little tiny receipt you're holding, and that's how you look at the plate.

819
01:00:23,675 --> 01:00:28,610
Speaker 0: You scan -- everything you do in this game is always scanning two barcodes.

820
01:00:29,090 --> 01:00:30,938
Speaker 0: Scan an action, and then scan a thing.

821
01:00:31,219 --> 01:00:32,625
Speaker 0: Scan an action, scan a thing.

822
01:00:32,665 --> 01:00:33,910
Speaker 0: That's all the input for the game.

823
01:00:34,571 --> 01:00:46,890
Speaker 0: And the most genius thing is that on that main, the main receipt printer, the big long one that's going infinitely, that has all the output from the game, when it prints the room you're in, right?

824
01:00:47,912 --> 01:00:50,729
Speaker 0: It actually prints out a little picture of the room you're in.

825
01:00:51,431 --> 01:00:57,070
Speaker 0: And if you look closely at it, you'll notice all the items in the room have barcodes on them.

826
01:00:57,170 --> 01:01:07,270
Speaker 0: So it's like when you open the fridge, you'll see there's, like, eggs in the fridge, and it's a picture of eggs, but the picture of the egg carton is filled in with a barcode.

827
01:01:07,611 --> 01:01:18,802
Speaker 0: So you click "look" -- well, you click, you scan the look barcode, and then you go over to the big receipt, the mega long one, and you scan the eggs in there, right?

828
01:01:19,364 --> 01:01:21,049
Speaker 0: And it's like, "Ah, yes, you see a carton of

829
01:01:21,149 --> 01:01:21,330
Speaker 0: eggs.".

830
01:01:21,891 --> 01:01:28,110
Speaker 0: And then you scan "take eggs," and then the tiny receipt printer prints out a little receipt with the eggs.

831
01:01:28,610 --> 01:01:36,010
Speaker 0: And so that way, you can go to a different room and still have the egg barcode available now to do something else with.

832
01:01:36,931 --> 01:01:50,020
Speaker 0: That's the whole game, but what shocked me the most, not only did they have this genius input system for a basic adventure game, but that it was functional and responsive and fast.

833
01:01:50,180 --> 01:01:54,850
Speaker 0: Like, you scanned, and it printed out quickly in shocking resolution.

834
01:01:55,150 --> 01:02:01,548
Speaker 0: It didn't run out of paper, it didn't run out of ink, maybe I just got to it when it was working well, but it was working so well.

835
01:02:02,691 --> 01:02:06,744
Speaker 0: Like, it was probably might even be faster than Shadowgate on the NES, right?

836
01:02:06,804 --> 01:02:08,589
Speaker 0: It's like beep beep, right?

837
01:02:09,970 --> 01:02:11,539
Speaker 0: You were going fast in this game.

838
01:02:11,559 --> 01:02:13,429
Speaker 0: It was an impressive game.

839
01:02:13,810 --> 01:02:15,544
Speaker 1: Just what an installation.

840
01:02:15,645 --> 01:02:16,270
Speaker 1: that set up for us.

841
01:02:16,270 --> 01:02:28,550
Speaker 0: It was made by Team Registration at WPI IMGD Questfast, designed and developed by Eddie, Garrett, Xian, Zihang Chen, and Lexi McFann in just over a week.

842
01:02:29,871 --> 01:02:31,278
Speaker 0: Took them over a week to make this.

843
01:02:31,761 --> 01:02:33,429
Speaker 0: Some genius kids over there at WPI.

844
01:02:34,892 --> 01:02:40,605
Speaker 1: So, I think we've gone on long enough, but suffice it to say, that was only a small sampling.

845
01:02:40,645 --> 01:02:41,146
Speaker 0: I got more.

